        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose opinion essays on broad topics. Interactive: Provide feedback on narrative writing.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose an opinion essay on Panama's role in international relations (e.g., "The Panama Canal strengthens global trade by reducing travel time between continents, making Panama a strategic trade partner and economic hub for international companies. Additionally, Panama's diplomatic efforts, such as hosting international summits, demonstrate its commitment to fostering cooperation and dialogue among nations.")
            Can edit narrative texts for clarity and style, providing feedback to peers related to Panama's global or national significance (e.g., "I think you can improve your story by adding more details about Panama's role in international trade.")
            Can offer feedback on essays about Panama's relevance and impact, focusing on structure and argument strength (e.g., "Your essay needs clearer topic sentences to guide the reader through your points.")
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students are shown a picture of the Panama Canal and a headline about an international summit held in Panama. They are asked to spend 3 minutes free-writing in their notebooks about "What comes to mind when you see these images in relation to Panama's global impact?" The teacher encourages them to use vocabulary they have learned in previous lessons related to 'trade', 'international', 'hub', 'strategic'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a sample introductory paragraph for an opinion essay about Panama's role in global trade, highlighting a strong thesis statement. The teacher reads it aloud, modeling the pronunciation of /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global" and stressing modal verbs like "must" (e.g., "Panama <span style="font-weight: bold;">must</span> maintain its <span style="font-weight: bold;">global</span> <span style="font-weight: bold;">trade</span> relevance.") and Wh- question words. The teacher points out the use of passive voice (e.g., "Panama is recognized for its trade influence") and a relative clause (e.g., "a strategic hub that connects continents").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main point or 'thesis' of this paragraph?" (Elicits understanding of opinion essay structure).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you identify a sentence where Panama is *acted upon* rather than *acting*? What grammar structure is that?" (Checks understanding of passive voice).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic excerpt from an opinion essay or an article from an international relations journal (adapted to B1.3) titled "Panama's Indispensable Role in a Connected World." The text includes examples of passive voice, modals of necessity (e.g., 'must', 'should'), and relative clauses, along with target vocabulary such as 'trade', 'relevance', 'hub', 'financial services', 'companies', 'strategic', 'impact', 'global', 'diverse'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Structure arguments in writing to acknowledge and effectively communicate viewpoints on Panama's global role.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Students read the essay excerpt quickly and identify the author's main argument about Panama's importance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match specific sentences from the text (e.g., "Panama is recognized for its biodiversity.") with the function they serve in the essay (e.g., "Introducing a fact," "Supporting an argument").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar identification:</b> Students identify and underline all instances of passive voice, modals of necessity, and relative clauses used in the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using passive voice to introduce Panama's role, modals of necessity in professional settings, and relative clauses to add information. Target vocabulary: 'trade', 'relevance', 'hub', 'financial services', 'companies', 'strategic', 'impact', 'global', 'diverse', 'recognized', 'attract', 'maintain', 'engage'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students complete sentences or transform active sentences into passive voice using given verbs and target vocabulary. They also fill in blanks with appropriate modals of necessity or complete sentences with relative clauses.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "The world recognizes Panama for its canal." → "Panama ____ (recognize) for its canal." (Passive voice)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "Professionals ____ (must) be proficient in English to present Panama globally." (Modal of necessity)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "Panama is a country. It connects continents." → "Panama is a country ____ connects continents." (Relative clause)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Paragraph Building):</b> Students are given a set of keywords (e.g., 'trade', 'financial services', 'global', 'strategic', 'attract', 'companies', 'impact') and a prompt like "Explain how Panama's economy impacts its international standing." They must write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) incorporating at least one passive construction, one modal of necessity, and one relative clause.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Sentence Review:</b> Students exchange their paragraphs from the previous activity with a partner. They read their partner's paragraph and identify if the target grammar structures (passive voice, modals, relative clauses) and vocabulary are used correctly and effectively. They highlight one sentence they liked and one sentence that could be improved, suggesting a specific change related to grammar, vocabulary, or clarity.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will compose the introductory paragraph and at least one body paragraph of an opinion essay on the prompt: "Discuss Panama's multifaceted importance in international relations, beyond just the Canal. What makes Panama a key player on the global stage?"</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product - Opinion Essay Draft (Introductory and first body paragraph).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instructions:</b> Students must clearly state their opinion in the introduction and support it with arguments in the body paragraph. They are required to use at least two instances of passive voice, two modals of necessity, and two relative clauses. They should also integrate a minimum of five previously learned vocabulary words (e.g., 'biodiversity', 'financial services', 'ecotourist', 'strategic', 'relevant', 'impact', 'challenges').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support, clarifying doubts, and reminding students to consider their audience for international communication.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer feedback using a structured checklist focusing on essay components and target language use. This will align with the interactive writing component of the learning outcome.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their drafted opinion essays (introduction and body paragraph) with a different peer. Using a provided feedback sheet, they will read their partner's essay and provide written comments on:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the thesis statement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Strength and coherence of the argument in the body paragraph.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of passive voice, modals of necessity, and relative clauses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggestions for improving overall clarity and style.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written feedback and suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify and comment on the specified grammatical structures and vocabulary in peer's work.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Constructive nature of feedback, using polite and respectful language (Pragmatic Competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One specific thing I learned today about writing an opinion essay is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One area where I still need to improve my essay writing is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when trying to express your opinions clearly and effectively in English?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did receiving feedback from your peer help you understand aspects of your writing that could be improved?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially concerning the focus on writing and peer feedback? Students seemed engaged with the structured practice and were able to apply the grammar points. The peer feedback was mostly constructive.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with complex sentence structures involving multiple relative clauses. More guided practice on combining ideas and using cohesive devices might be needed. Ensure a clear rubric for peer assessment is provided next time to guide feedback more precisely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their opinion essays for a brief presentation, integrating the feedback received today, and engaging in a collaborative project to consolidate their understanding and communication skills related to Panama's importance. They will be tasked with transforming their written arguments into a concise oral summary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>